    "title": "ci: Pin actions by hash, limit token access",
    "body": "The workflow sets no `permissions:`, `actions/checkout` leaves the job's token in `.git/config`, and every action is referenced by a tag its owner can move. A moved tag is how tj-actions/changed-files ran code in March 2025 that printed CI secrets to public logs ([GHSA-mrrh-fwg8-r2c3](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mrrh-fwg8-r2c3)).\n\nNothing is exploitable today: the token is already read-only (the job logs show `Contents: read`, `Metadata: read`, `Packages: read`) and no step writes with it. Setting `permissions: contents: read` and `persist-credentials: false`, and pinning each action to its tag's commit, makes that explicit, for forks too.\n\nWould you take this? I have a PR ready. libsecp256k1 wants to stay in sync with Core here (bitcoin-core/secp256k1#1959), and I am opening the same change there.\n",
