      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "body": "Not sure about this, as mentioned previously: Container and GitHub Action pins are not done, because they:\n      - delay normal and security updates until someone bumps the pin;\n      - require recurring manual maintenance;\n      - are not recursive, so they can create false confidence about reproducibility and security\n\nSo there is something done in the name of \"security\", but there is a valid risk of actually decreasing \"security\".\n\n> in March 2025 that printed CI secrets\n\nI am sure that even with pinned actions, anyone is still free to print CI secrets, if they want:\n\n* GitHub itself (they can already do whatever they want)\n* The provider for the CI machines (they provide the hardware, so they can do whatever they want)\n* Any action that is pinned, by just doing the printing in a mutable included action\n* Likely any other ci package/dependency inside the container can do printing (assuming the secret makes it into the container)\n* ... etc\n\n\nIf we are worried about CI printing secrets, the solution is not to pin. The solution is not to provide any secrets.\n\n\n> Nothing is exploitable today: the token is already read-only (the job logs show `Contents: read`, `Metadata: read`, `Packages: read`) and no step writes with it. Setting `permissions: contents: read` and `persist-credentials: false`\n\nSeems fine. This was my criticisms about moving to GHA, and I am not familiar with their permission system for credentials. Though, if there is a reason/benefit making this stricter, it seems fine. But other people more familiar with GHA permissions would have to review that.",
